  "body": "Hands played: 57\nHand 57: Hero SB folded 7h2h; villain BB held 7c8c (suited connector).\n\nKey tendencies:\n- SB raise range WIDE: AdJd, JhTd, A6o, KsTo (4 observed) — not just premiums.\n- SB limp range: extremely wide, near-trash included (6hQo, Td8h, Js5s, 6h5s, Kh8d, Qd6d, Qs5h, 9s4c, 9cTh, Tc8d). ANY two cards.\n- SB call range: includes weak suited connectors/one-gappers like 6cQc, 9dJs.\n- SB fold preflop: 3 times (H14, H20, H32).\n- Folds to flop c-bet: 4/5 (80%).\n- Folds to ANY postflop hero bet eventually: 9/9 (100%) when villain checks first.\n- Villain postflop aggression: 10 total bets/raises. 3x made hand, 6x strong draw semi-bluff, 1x pure bluff (H54: 9cTh on dry KK5).\n- BOARD TEXTURE MATTERS for villain bets:\n    * WET/CONNECTED boards: villain likely has made hand OR strong draw (~6+ outs). Respect the bet.\n    * DRY/PAIRED boards: villain may be pure bluffing with air. Consider calling with showdown value.\n- NO SIZING TELL: Villain uses same small bet sizing for draws AND made hands AND bluffs.\n- When villain CHECKS any street, he is weak — fire freely.\n- When villain LEADS on wet board, respect it — he has made hand OR strong draw. Hero should call with strong made hands.\n- When villain LEADS on dry/paired board, he may be bluffing — consider calling with any showdown value (pair, even top-kicker).\n- BB range is extremely wide: Ts2s, 5d7s, 4sAc, Ks5d, 4d9s, Td6c, 6h5c, 5h5d, 7dKh, Jc3h, Qd7d, 2d6h, AcAs, 6dJd, 6hQc, 2s8d, 8cTc, 5c6h, 6hTs, 8h8d, 6sQs, 8hQd, 2dJh, 3d4c, 9dTs, Kd9c, 7c8c.\n- CRITICAL EXPLOITATION: Hero massively over-folding SB — folded A3o (H55), 33 (H53!), A9o (H47!!), JJ (H41!), KAo (H35), KdAd (H5), TT (H19), Ad6c, Ad4s, Ah3c, 7h2h (H57 — ok). Hero MUST open any hand worth playing from SB.\n- CRITICAL EXPLOITATION 2: On dry boards, villain bluffs — hero should call/float with any real showdown value. On wet boards, be more cautious with marginal hands vs villain's bets.",
